{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c6e9","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0c36e-d989-7603-9e05-f0fee9b1e516"},"sequence":463,"recorded_at":1780531400000921,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"78e4a4f8-26f7-4967-82c0-c54272cc4623","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"78e4a4f8-26f7-4967-82c0-c54272cc4623","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"78e4a4f8-26f7-4967-82c0-c54272cc4623"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0c370-abe6-7e62-8c79-91712d6f2267"},"task_id":"01a0c370-abe6-7e62-8c79-91712d6f2267","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0c370-abe6-7e62-8c79-91712d6f2267","chunk":"Read text file `internal/secprim/nofollow.go`.\n1|package secprim\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"errors\"\n5|\t\"os\"\n6|\t\"strings\"\n7|)\n8|\n9|// errIsSymlink reports the Windows Lstat gate refusal. It stays internal:\n10|// callers see the failPath refusal, and errors.Is against ErrUnsafePath\n11|// is the supported match.\n12|var errIsSymlink = errors.New(\"secprim symlink refused before open\")\n13|\n14|// errCommitSymlink is the internal signal from the platform commit walk\n15|// (openCommitRelative) that a symlink or reparse point stands on the\n16|// member path at commit time. It stays internal: Open maps it to the\n17|// failPath \"member symlink escape\" refusal, and errors.Is against\n18|// ErrUnsafePath is the supported match. The walk returns no failPath\n19|// sites of its own so the refusal inventory stays in shared code,\n20|// which the suite executes on every platform it runs on.\n21|var errCommitSymlink = errors.New(\"secprim symlink on the commit path\")\n22|\n23|// guardRootMatches reports whether the open directory handle refers to\n24|// the same directory as rootPath: the handle's fstat identity against a\n25|// stat of the path (device and inode on unix via os.SameFile, volume\n26|// and file index on Windows). A stat failure on either side is not a\n27|// match: an unreadable root fails closed into the mismatch refusal\n28|// rather than admitting an unverifiable handle.\n29|func guardRootMatches(root *os.File, rootPath string) bool {\n30|\thandleInfo, err := root.Stat()\n31|\tif err != nil || handleInfo == nil {\n32|\t\treturn false\n33|\t}\n34|\tpathInfo, err := os.Stat(rootPath)\n35|\tif err != nil || pathInfo == nil {\n36|\t\treturn false\n37|\t}\n38|\treturn os.SameFile(handleInfo, pathInfo)\n39|}\n40|\n41|// OpenNoFollowFile opens an existing regular file read-only, refusing a\n42|// trailing symlink instead of traversing it (Section 16.3\n43|// \"symlink/reparse escape during both validation and commit\"). The\n44|// descriptor refers to the opened inode, and the post-open Fstat below\n45|// reads that descriptor rather than the path, so a swap between open and\n46|// stat cannot retarget the check: only the final-component open itself\n47|// races, and on unix O_NOFOLLOW closes even that.\n48|//\n49|// The file must be regular: directories, devices, pipes, and sockets are\n50|// refused after opening (and the descriptor closed) rather than handed\n51|// back. Parent directories are the caller's responsibility: open the\n52|// staging root with OpenNoFollowDir and commit members through\n53|// Guard.Open, which binds the walk to the root handle. Composing\n54|// Guard.Resolve with this function instead is not contained: Resolve\n55|// answers the lexical question only, and a symlinked intermediate\n56|// directory redirects a path-string open outside the root.\n57|func OpenNoFollowFile(path string) (*os.File, error) {\n58|\tfile, err := openNoFollow(path)\n59|\tif err != nil {\n60|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"open failed\", memberErrorTarget(path)).WithCause(err)\n61|\t}\n62|\tinfo, err := statOpenedFile(file)\n63|\tif err != nil {\n64|\t\t_ = file.Close()\n65|\t\treturn nil, failPath(\"open stat failed\", memberErrorTarget(path)).WithCause(err)\n66|\t}\n67|\tif err := CheckRegularTarget(info); err != nil {\n68|\t\t_ = file.Close()\n69|\t\treturn nil, err\n70|\t}\n71|\t// The unix open sets O_NONBLOCK so a FIFO can never stall the opener\n72|\t// (its refusal above runs only after the open returns). A regular\n73|\t// file ignores O_NONBLOCK on reads, and clearing it here keeps a\n74|\t// borrowed descriptor from leaking the flag to its reader. fcntl on\n75|\t// a just-opened valid descriptor cannot fail; the best-effort clear\n76|\t// carries no refusal site for that reason.\n77|\t_ = clearNonblock(file)\n78|\treturn file, nil\n79|}\n80|\n81|// Open commits one guard member to an open regular file through the\n82|// staging-root directory handle, refusing symlink and reparse escape at\n83|// commit time (Section 16.3 \"symlink/reparse escape during both\n84|// validation and commit\").\n85|//\n86|// Validation and commit are two halves of one entry point, not two calls\n87|// the caller composes: the member grammar and the managed set refuse\n88|// lexically first, then the platform walk (openCommitRelative) opens\n89|// every path component relative to a directory descriptor — never by\n90|// re-resolving a path string — so a symlinked intermediate directory\n91|// cannot redirect the commit outside the root the way composing\n92|// Guard.Resolve with OpenNoFollowFile does (O_NOFOLLOW constrains only\n93|// the final component; every component before it is still followed by\n94|// the kernel when a path string is opened).\n95|//\n96|// The caller opens the staging root with OpenNoFollowDir and passes the\n97|// handle: on unix the walk never leaves that handle (openat from the\n98|// root descriptor down, O_NOFOLLOW per component, TOCTOU-closed); on\n99|// Windows the walk is a best-effort Lstat component check with the\n100|// stated check-then-open bound. The returned file is cleared of\n(more lines below)","final_result":true}}}
